-- #1305 NON-REGRESSION + COVERAGE CELL for the removal of the `DNewtype` arm from
-- `backend/private_mangle.ctorExportEntries`.
--
-- That arm offered an importing unit the symbol `<nmodw>__Wrapw` for a constructor
-- the front end never puts in that unit's scope.  This program is the case that must
-- keep working across the removal: a newtype constructor that IS legitimately live —
-- used inside its own module, and colliding by spelling with a public data
-- constructor another module genuinely exports.
--
-- Two different peers are in play and deleting the wrong one is the trap.  The
-- ctor-EXPORT index answers "may an IMPORTER name this?" (the arm that was removed);
-- `unitLocalCtorNames` answers "does this unit define it?" and is deliberately
-- unfiltered, which is what still renames nmodw's `Wrapw` to `nmodw__Wrapw` at its
-- definition and at both uses inside nmodw.
--
-- Expected output, derived by hand BEFORE any capture and independently of any
-- engine: `Wrapw` in THIS file can only be bmodw's arity-2 constructor — nmodw is
-- imported without `(..)` and without a `Wrapw` member, so it contributes no
-- constructor here — giving `unBW (Wrapw 1 3)` = `1 + 3` = `4`.  nmodw's own `Wrapw`
-- is reached only through `mkNTW`/`unNTW`, which is the only way it is reachable at
-- all, giving `9`.  So `9` then `4`.
--
-- ⚠️ WHY nmodw's CONSTRUCTOR IS NOT IMPORTED HERE: it cannot be.  Measured on this
-- binary with no collision present, `import nmodw.{NTW(..), unNTW}`,
-- `import nmodw.{NTW, Wrapw, unNTW}` and `import nmodw.*` all give
-- `T-UNBOUND` `./p.mdk:3:23: Unbound variable: Wrapw`.  With the collision present,
-- adding the `(..)` instead gets a RESOLVE refusal — `Ambiguous constructor: 'Wrapw'
-- is brought into scope by both `nmodw` and `bmodw`` — so no spelling reaches the
-- emitter with both constructors live.  That is why the shape #1305 actually files
-- (a collider the unit never imports BY NAME, so no ambiguity is detected) cannot be
-- expressed here and stays in `test/must_fail_fixtures/`.
--
-- 🚨 THIS IS NOT A FAIL-CAPABLE REGRESSION GUARD FOR THE SHIPPED DIFF, AND THE
-- ATTEMPTS ARE RECORDED SO NOBODY CITES IT AS ONE.  It is green on pristine bfee53b4
-- and green after the change, because the change only DELETES an export-index entry
-- that no correct program could use.  Three counterfactual binaries were built and
-- measured, not reasoned about:
--   * `localCtorNames`'s `DNewtype` arm deleted (the plausible over-reach when "drop
--     the newtype arm" is read as one instruction instead of two) — this program
--     still built and still printed `9` / `4`, exit 0.  Derived reason: nmodw's
--     `Wrapw` then stays bare on BOTH its definition and its uses, while bmodw's is
--     still mangled, so the two never collide in the flat ctor table.
--   * the same mutation against TWO modules whose newtype constructors share a
--     spelling (`NTW = Wrapw Int` / `CTW = Wrapw Bool`) — also still correct, `9` /
--     `1`.  A newtype constructor is always arity 1, so unmangling one cannot
--     produce the differing-arity collapse the mangler exists to prevent.
--   * pristine bfee53b4 — `9` / `4`, exit 0.
-- So: treat this as a COVERAGE cell, not as evidence the change was necessary.  The
-- two cells that ARE fail-capable for this PR are the sibling
-- `ctor_scope_ctor_member_wild_suffix/` and `ctor_scope_ctor_member_wild_dual_role/`,
-- both measured RED on pristine bfee53b4.
--
-- ⚠️ IT IS THIS CORPUS'S FIRST `newtype` AT ALL.  Before it, `grep -rn '\bnewtype\b'
-- test/llvm_fixtures_modules/` returned nothing, so no native gate in the tree
-- exercised newtype constructor mangling across a module boundary.
import nmodw.{NTW, mkNTW, unNTW}
import bmodw.{BW, Wrapw, unBW}

main : <IO> Unit
main =
  let _ = putStrLn (intToString (unNTW (mkNTW 9)))
  putStrLn (intToString (unBW (Wrapw 1 3)))
